Sentiment Analysis Model

Introduction

This model is a natural language processing (NLP) algorithm designed to identify, extract, and quantify the emotional tone of written text. Specifically, this model performs binary sentiment classification, categorizing text as either Positive (1) or Negative (0).How It Categorizes Text

Sentiment analysis models typically classify text into specific buckets depending on their configuration:
Polarity-based (Most Common): Classifies text as Positive, Negative, or Neutral.
Fine-grained: Offers nuance by scaling sentiment (e.g., Very Positive, Positive, Neutral, Negative, Very Negative).
Emotion Detection: Identifies specific states like Anger, Joy, Sadness, Surprise, or Frustration.
Aspect-Based (Advanced): Pinpoints specific features (e.g., "camera quality" vs. "battery life") and their associated sentiment.
Model Architecture

The data pipeline processes text through the following stages:

Input Text → Tokenizer → Embedding (300-dim) → BiLSTM (256 hidden units) → Max Pooling → Dense Layer (128 → ReLU) → Softmax Output (2 classes)Data Flow
Embedding Layer: Raw text is tokenized and converted into dense numerical vectors (using Word2Vec or GloVe).
BiLSTM Layer: Processes text in two directions (Forward for past context, Backward for future context) to capture semantic relationships.
Pooling Layer: Compresses sequences into a single vector (Max Pooling captures dominant features; Average Pooling captures aggregated tone).
Classification Layer: Uses a Fully Connected (Dense) layer with Softmax/Sigmoid activation to output probability scores.
